    <!-- FAQ answers: \\n between bullets; use ** only for mode names worth scanning. -->
    <string name="faq_answer_rule_not_working">Give the rule a name, source folders, destination folder, and file types.\nTap any red folder path on the edit screen and grant access again.\nIf the rule uses Download folder or a storage root, switch to **All files access** in Settings.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_folder_access_denied">Common after changing **Selective Access** vs **All files access**, restoring a backup, or revoking a folder grant.\nOpen Edit rule, tap the highlighted path, and pick the folder again in the folder picker.\nDownload folder and storage roots need **All files access**.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_cant_add_downloads_folder">In **Selective Access**, Android often blocks the Download folder and some storage roots.\nTurn on **All files access** in Settings, then add the folder, or pick a subfolder inside Download with the picker.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_files_not_moving_automatically">Confirm the rule is enabled.\nFor automatic runs, set a schedule and keep the rule enabled.\nCheck folder access, battery restrictions for FilePipe, and advanced filters so files still match.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_create_rule">Tap + on the Rules tab to start a new rule.\nPick source folders, file types, and a destination folder.\nChoose operation mode (move or copy) and how to handle duplicate files in the destination.\nAdd a schedule on the edit screen if you want background runs.\nUse Advanced filters for filename patterns, size or age limits, and exclude patterns.\nSave, then run Preview to see which files would be affected.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_rule_skipping_files">The file may not match the extensions or advanced filters.\nIf the destination already has that name, your conflict setting may skip or rename instead of replacing.\nLost folder access can also hide files until you grant again.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_what_preview_does">Preview lists files the rule would act on and shows the intended destination path.\nNothing is moved or copied until you run the rule for real.\nUse it to check filters, destination, and access before you rely on the rule.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_scheduled_rules">Scheduled rules run in the background at the times you configure.\nThey still need valid folder access and files that match the rule.\nUse Preview after changes so automation matches what you expect.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_rule_did_not_run">Confirm the schedule and the rule are both enabled.\nAndroid battery limits and notification settings can block or delay background work.\nRecheck folder access and that matching files still exist.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_schedule_notifications">FilePipe doesn\'t send a notification after every scheduled run. Check Run History to see what happened.\nTo get alerts, enable run notifications in Settings and allow app notifications in Android settings.\nBattery saver and background restrictions can delay runs — exclude FilePipe in battery settings if needed.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_data_privacy">FilePipe works on your device.\nYour files are not uploaded or tracked.\nThe app only runs the rules you configure.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_why_storage_access">FilePipe needs access to the folders your rules use to scan, move, or copy files.\n**Selective Access** only sees folders you pick in the system dialog.\n**All files access** is needed for Download, storage roots, and plain file paths.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_backup_contents">Backups include rules, run history, and settings.\nAndroid does not restore folder grants from a backup.\nAfter restore, revisit rules with highlighted paths and grant again.</string>
    <string name="faq_answer_permissions_after_restore">For **Selective Access**, tap each affected folder and grant again.\nIf rules used **All files access**, turn that back on in Android settings.\nHighlights on the edit screen show which folders still need attention.</string>

